# Settings for nifty-spot-intraday-collector.
#
# Copy this file, then fill in the values below:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Or don't: the launcher (`python3 launcher/server.py`) writes this file for
# you, with every field labelled and explained on screen. This file is for
# people who'd rather edit it by hand.
#
# You do NOT need to `source` this file. It's read automatically every time
# you run anything. Note that a variable you've already exported in your
# shell always wins over what's written here.
#
# There are five settings, and this is the only place they live -- no other
# config file, nothing hidden elsewhere. Full guide: docs/CREDENTIALS.md
#
# ABOUT BROKERS: this collects NIFTY 50 data; the broker is a replaceable
# part of it. Upstox is the only broker wired up in this build, which is why
# these settings are named UPSTOX_* -- that's what today's code reads.


# ===========================================================================
# 1. WHERE YOUR DATA GOES
# ===========================================================================

# The database file. It doesn't need to exist yet -- it's created for you on
# the first collection. Use a full path, for example:
#
#     SPOT_DB_PATH=/Users/you/market-data/spot.db
#     SPOT_DB_PATH=/home/you/market-data/spot.db
#     SPOT_DB_PATH=C:/Users/you/market-data/spot.db
#
# Leave it blank and every command refuses to run, on purpose. There is no
# default path, because a default here caused real damage in the system this
# project came from: it quietly created an empty database somewhere nobody
# expected, and runs against it reported "0 rows" while looking exactly like
# success. Refusing to guess is the whole point.
#
# Using Docker? Ignore this. docker-compose.yml always sets it to
# /data/spot.db inside the container, whatever you put here.
SPOT_DB_PATH=


# ===========================================================================
# 2. YOUR BROKER'S APP KEYS  --  set these once
# ===========================================================================
# You get these by registering an app on your broker's developer site. It
# takes about 10 minutes, and docs/CREDENTIALS.md walks through it. They
# rarely change after that.

# Your broker calls this "API Key".
#
# Yes, the names differ -- your broker says API Key, this setting says
# CLIENT_ID. Same value. That mismatch is the single thing that confused
# testers most, so it's worth stating twice.
#
# On Upstox: Apps -> Algo Trading tab -> expand your app's row.
# Format: a UUID, like f5f5f626-226f-41ad-8ee9-42dab7e53273
UPSTOX_CLIENT_ID=

# Your broker calls this "API Secret". Same screen as the API Key, shown
# masked with a reveal button.
#
# Treat it like a password. Anyone with your Key and Secret together can
# impersonate your app.
UPSTOX_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Where your broker sends your browser back to after you log in.
#
# It must match what you registered with your broker CHARACTER FOR
# CHARACTER, or the login is rejected outright.
#
# Which value you want depends on how you connect:
#
#   Using the launcher     http://127.0.0.1:8765/callback
#                          (a real address -- the launcher is listening on
#                          it, which is how it catches the login for you.
#                          Change the port here if you changed it there.)
#
#   Using the command line https://127.0.0.1/callback
#                          (a placeholder -- nothing listens on it. Your
#                          browser shows an error page after login, which is
#                          expected, and you copy the address bar back into
#                          the terminal.)
UPSTOX_REDIRECT_URI=


# ===========================================================================
# 3. TODAY'S ACCESS TOKEN  --  usually leave this blank
# ===========================================================================

# This is the one that expires every morning.
#
# Most people leave this empty. Connecting through the launcher, or running
# `nifty-spot auth`, saves your daily token to a separate credentials file
# and it's picked up automatically from there.
#
# Fill it in only if you're copying a token straight from your broker's
# dashboard instead of logging in (Upstox shows one on the same screen as
# your API Key).
#
# WATCH OUT: a token written here ALWAYS wins over one saved by logging in,
# with no staleness check and no warning. So if you paste one here once and
# later switch to logging in, this stale value silently overrides every
# fresh token -- and it looks like "logging in worked but collecting still
# fails", the next day, for a reason that has nothing to do with logging in.
# If you ever paste a token here, blank it out again once you're done.
# `./verify.sh` tells you which source your token actually came from.
UPSTOX_ACCESS_TOKEN=
